<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris I of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 852–889; d. <a href="/years/907">907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saint Boris, Boris-Michael, Saint Boris I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris I was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from 852 to 889. He accepted Christian baptism around 864, taking the name Michael, and secured an autonomous Bulgarian church under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 886 he welcomed the disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, whose schools at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> developed Slavonic literature. He retired to a monastery, briefly returning in 893 to depose his son Vladimir, and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-i-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Boris I of Bulgaria" width="250" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_I_of_Bulgaria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boris was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presian-i">Presian I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. In 852 he sent emissaries to Eastern Francia to confirm the peace of 845. In 854 the Moravian prince Rastislav persuaded him to help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, and the campaign was a disaster: Louis invaded Bulgaria, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> also attacked. Peace was restored in 855, but meanwhile a conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, begun in 855 or 856, cost him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> (Plovdiv), the region of Zagora and the ports of the Gulf of Burgas. An invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 853 or 854, led by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-rasate">Vladimir-Rasate</a>, was beaten by Mutimir's army, and Boris then made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Late in 863 the Byzantine emperor Michael III declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> during a famine and natural disasters. Boris made peace, promising to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> by the eastern rite in return for territorial concessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and at the start of 864 he was secretly baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> with Michael III as godfather, taking the name Michael. Historians give several reasons: diplomacy, the influence of a sister baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the wish to unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> warrior aristocracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, who had different beliefs. In the summer of 865 a group of boyars rose in revolt, and Boris suppressed it and executed 52 boyars with their families.</p><p>Boris sought an autocephalous church, and in August 866 sent 106 questions to Pope Nicholas I. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> refused to name Formosus archbishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> turned back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and at the Fourth Council of Constantinople in 870 its church was made an autocephalous archbishopric. In 886 his governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> received the exiled disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, and Boris welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a> and Naum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>. They set up centres of teaching and trained Slavonic-speaking priests to replace the Greek clergy, and in 893 the Cyrillic script and Old Bulgarian were declared official.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 889 Boris abdicated and became a monk. His son Vladimir tried to restore paganism, which brought Boris out of retirement in 893. He defeated Vladimir, had him blinded, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-preslav">Council of Preslav</a> placed his third son Simeon I on the throne. About 895 he emerged again to help Simeon against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>. He died on 2 May 907, and where he was buried is uncertain; it may be near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> or at a monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> venerates him as a saint, the baptiser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Equal-to-the-Apostles, with a feast on 2 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boris I of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early Bulgars worshipped the sky god Tangra, while the Slavs kept their own pagan cults, until <b>Boris I</b> accepted baptism from Byzantium in 864 and imposed Christianity on his people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the reigns of Presian and <b>Boris I</b> in the mid-9th century their territory passed to the First Bulgarian Empire, and the name disappears.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Veliki Preslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=879" aria-label="Pliska on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5203 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Boris I of Bulgaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5653 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5653 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=879">What was here in 879 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
